{"name":"Green Bonnet","hex":"#8bb490","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"bonnet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of hat, once widely worn by women or children, held in place by ribbons tied under the chin; now uncommonly worn except in certain religious sects.","A traditional Scottish woollen brimless cap; a bunnet."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put a bonnet on.","To take off the bonnet or cap as a mark of respect; to uncover."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bonnet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bonnet (headgear)","blocks":[{"text":"Bonnet has been used as the name for a wide variety of headgear for women since the Middle Ages. As with \"hat\" and \"cap\", it is impossible to generalize styles for which the word has been used, there is a tendency to describe it having soft material and lacking a brim, or at least one all the way round, rather than just at the front. Yet the term has also been used, for example, for steel helmets in Scotland (1505), where it has long been especially popular."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Old_woman_in_sunbonnet_by_Doris_Ulmann.jpg/330px-Old_woman_in_sunbonnet_by_Doris_Ulmann.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonnet_(headgear)","wikidata":"Q1149531"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/M%C3%B6ssa_av_spets_och_tyll_med_knytband%2C_fr%C3%A5n_1880-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0053084.jpg/500px-M%C3%B6ssa_av_spets_och_tyll_med_knytband%2C_fr%C3%A5n_1880-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0053084.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/M%C3%B6ssa_av_spets_och_tyll_med_knytband%2C_fr%C3%A5n_1880-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0053084.jpg/500px-M%C3%B6ssa_av_spets_och_tyll_med_knytband%2C_fr%C3%A5n_1880-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0053084.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A bonnet decorated with lace and tulle from the 1880s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM%C3%B6ssa_av_spets_och_tyll_med_knytband%2C_fr%C3%A5n_1880-talet_-_Nordiska_Museet_-_NMA.0053084.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/JaneAustenCassandraW